n the one hand uh you know the threat  of nuclear attack but on the other hand  both sides agreeing to talk jake let's  look at the front of the guardian putin  puts nuclear force on harlot as backlash  grows that huge demonstration uh in  berlin  what are your thoughts is this nuclear  threat in order to divert attention  because he has miscalculated this  invasion  well i think in a normal in normal times  one would say that a country like russia  would make a nuclear threat really as a  bluff but if you rewind a couple of  weeks everyone thought that putin was  bluffing when he put more than 100 000  troops on the border of ukraine nobody  really thought that he was going to take  the step of invading and so i think that  that makes adds a sense of jeopardy to  these uh nuclear threats or the idea of  him putting nuclear forces on on high  alert i think it's important to drill  down to what's actually going on here  putin is referring to a 2020  announcement by russia a new policy  announcement which was that it would  reserve the right to use nuclear weapons  in retaliation when facing an  existential threat from conventional  weapons uh from an aggressor uh whether  that's the west or nato whether that's  ukraine or another country  and you know it's it's not clear whether  that means the the use of tactical  nuclear weapons which is nuclear weapons  on a small scale in warfare which we've  never seen before in history or  something bigger so there's a sort of  intentional vagary here that's been used  strategically by putin to try to instill  fear into the ukrainians and into uh you  know nato and the west at large um but  it seems like at the moment what is  really evoking is a stronger backlash by  the ukraine and indeed by western powers  as well  yeah and sebastian just going back to  your paper the financial times clearly  putin has been rattled by uh the  sanctions hasn't he and i see that uh  the front of the ft talking about uh bp  unloading nearly 20 